<task>
    Extract the Apple device configuration described in a classified-ad HTML
    snippet, and return it as a single JSON object. Follow the &lt;procedure&gt;
    exactly — it is a fixed checklist, not optional.
</task>

<input>
    HTML_CONTENT:
    {{adHtmlContent}}

    Read only the ad's title, main description text, and any structured
    spec/attribute fields. Ignore CSS, &lt;script&gt; blocks, nav/menu chrome,
    and images.

    STOP READING at the first occurrence of any of these section markers
    (and anything after them): "Das könnte dich auch interessieren",
    "Weitere Anzeigen für dich", "Mehr von diesem Anbieter", or close
    variants. Do this filtering FIRST, before extraction.
</input>

<reference_data>
    See attached device catalog JSON. Each device row has an `id` and allowed
    value lists: `silicon[]`, `colors[]`, `storage[]`, `unified_memory[]`,
    each with their own `id`.
</reference_data>

<procedure>
    Work through these steps in order. Keep work internal; output only the
    final JSON.

    STEP 1 — Locate the device family.
    Scan for an explicit Model Number (format A####, e.g. "A2337"). If found,
    that uniquely fixes `model_id` — skip to STEP 3 for that row only.
    If absent, narrow by product line + size + generation hints (e.g.
    "MacBook Pro 14 Zoll", "iPad Air", release year, "Touch Bar" vs no
    Touch Bar). Shortlist every catalog row that's still plausible.

    STEP 2 — Pick the primary row.
    From the shortlist, choose the single most likely row using context
    (typical default configs, release recency, popularity). Put it in
    `model_id`. Put the rest of the shortlist into `alternative_candidates`,
    most-plausible first. If the shortlist had only one row, leave
    `alternative_candidates` as [].

    STEP 3 — Extract raw attribute text.
    Pull out, verbatim from the ad, anything resembling: chip name/generation
    (M1–M5, A-series, plus Pro/Max/Ultra variant), CPU core count, GPU core
    count, color name, storage size, unified memory size. Don't map to IDs
    yet — just capture the text.

    STEP 4 — Normalize and map to IDs (within the chosen row only).
    Before comparing chip text to `silicon[]` entries, normalize both sides:
    lowercase, treat "-", "‑" (U+2011), and "–" as equivalent, and treat
    "mit"/"und" as equivalent to "with"/"and"/"+". A chip match requires:
    same chip generation (M4 ≠ M5), same Pro/Max/Ultra tier, AND matching
    CPU-core and GPU-core numbers.
    For each of silicon_id / color_id / storage_id / memory_id:
    - Special rule: If the device only has one possible silicon use that silicon.
    - If the normalized ad text matches an entry in that row's allowed
    list → use that entry's id.
    - If the ad gives a value but it does NOT appear in the row's allowed
    list → this is a signal STEP 2 may have picked the wrong row.
    Re-check STEP 2 before finalizing; if still no match, set to null.
    - If the ad simply never mentions that attribute → null.
    Never output an id that isn't in the matched row's own list (hard
    constraint — this overrides any other instinct).

    STEP 4a — Storage vs. memory disambiguation (German ads).
    Ads are normally in German. Storage and memory are both expressed as
    plain &lt;number&gt; values, so do not rely on magnitude or position
    alone — anchor on keywords first:
    - STORAGE keywords: "SSD", "Festplatte", "interner Speicher",
    "Speicherplatz", "Speicherkapazität". Storage may be given in GB
    (256GB, 512GB) or TB (1TB, 2TB, 4TB, 8TB) — TB is ALWAYS storage,
    never memory.
    - MEMORY keywords: "RAM", "Arbeitsspeicher", "Hauptspeicher",
    "Unified Memory", "Memory". Memory is always given in GB, never TB.
    - Combined shorthand is common, e.g. "8/256GB", "16/512GB", or
    "256GB / 16GB RAM". If a keyword is attached to at least one of
    the two numbers, use it to anchor that one, then assign the
    remaining number to the other attribute. Only fall back to the
    conventional "RAM/Storage" ordering (first number = memory,
    second = storage) when NEITHER number has a keyword to anchor on
    — and reflect that extra uncertainty in STEP 6's confidence score.
    - Some sizes exist in BOTH the storage and memory allowed lists for
    a given row (e.g. 64GB and 128GB can be either, depending on the
    Pro/Max configuration). A bare number with no keyword and no
    paired second number is NOT safe to assign — leave it null rather
    than guessing which field it belongs to.
    - Normalize formatting before matching: "GB"/"Gb"/"gb"/"Giga"/
    "Gigabyte" are equivalent, and ignore spaces between number and
    unit ("512 GB" = "512GB"). Treat "1TB"/"2TB"/etc. as the matching
    catalog TB entry directly — don't convert it to a GB figure.

    STEP 5 — iPad cellular rule.
    If the matched row is an iPad and the ad text contains no explicit
    cellular indicator ("LTE", "5G", "Cellular", "SIM"), prefer the Wi‑Fi-only
    sibling row over the Wi‑Fi+Cellular row.

    STEP 6 — Confidence.
    - 8–10: Model Number was explicit (STEP 1 shortcut), OR every extracted
    attribute mapped unambiguously with zero competing rows.
    - 4–7: Device class is clear but model_id required inference (STEP 2),
    OR alternative_candidates is non-empty, OR one or more attribute ids
    are null due to genuine ambiguity/silence in the ad.
    - 1–3: Ad text is sparse, contradictory, or chaotic enough that the
    device identity itself is in real doubt.

    STEP 7 — Seller identity.
    Look in the ad body, profile snippet, or metadata for a name/username.
    "Privatanbieter: Max Mustermann" → first="Max", last="Mustermann".
    A single name/handle → first_name only, last_name="N/A".
    Nothing found → both "N/A".
</procedure>